# Drag

Any motion component can be made draggable with the `drag` prop. The component gets full physics-based momentum, elastic constraints, and gesture callbacks.

```svelte
<motion.div drag />
```

<Example isSmall>
  <DragExample />
</Example>

## Usage

Set `drag` to `true` to enable dragging on both axes:

```svelte
<motion.div drag />
```

To restrict dragging to a single axis, pass `"x"` or `"y"`:

```svelte
<motion.div drag="x" />
```

## Constraints

Drag movement can be constrained with the `dragConstraints` prop. Constraints can be defined as pixel offsets from the element's origin, or as a reference to another element.

### Pixel constraints

Pass an object with `top`, `left`, `right`, and `bottom` values (in pixels):

```svelte
<motion.div
    drag
    dragConstraints={{ left: -100, right: 100, top: -50, bottom: 50 }}
/>
```

### Element constraints

Pass an element reference to constrain dragging within that element's bounding box:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { motion } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    let bounds: HTMLElement | null = null
</script>

<div bind:this={bounds}>
    <motion.div drag dragConstraints={bounds} />
</div>
```

<Example isSmall>
  <DragConstraintsExample />
</Example>

## Elastic

By default, dragging beyond constraints has an elastic feel. Control this with `dragElastic`:

```svelte
<!-- No elasticity — hard stop at constraints -->
<motion.div drag dragConstraints={{ left: -100, right: 100 }} dragElastic={0} />

<!-- Full elasticity (default ~0.35) -->
<motion.div drag dragConstraints={{ left: -100, right: 100 }} dragElastic={1} />
```

Set `dragElastic` to `0` for a hard boundary, or `1` for maximum stretch. Values between `0` and `1` control the amount of elastic overdrag.

## Momentum

After releasing a drag, the element continues with momentum by default. Disable this with `dragMomentum`:

```svelte
<motion.div drag dragMomentum={false} />
```

Fine-tune the physics with `dragTransition`:

```svelte
<motion.div
    drag
    dragTransition={{
        bounceStiffness: 300,
        bounceDamping: 20,
        power: 0.8,
        timeConstant: 200
    }}
/>
```

## Snap to origin

Set `dragSnapToOrigin` to spring the element back to its starting position on release:

```svelte
<motion.div
    drag
    dragSnapToOrigin
    whileDrag={{ scale: 1.05, cursor: 'grabbing' }}
/>
```

## Direction lock

When `dragDirectionLock` is enabled and both axes are allowed, the drag locks to whichever axis the user moves first (threshold: 4px):

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { motion } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    let lockedAxis = $state<'x' | 'y' | null>(null)
</script>

<motion.div
    drag
    dragDirectionLock
    onDirectionLock={(axis) => (lockedAxis = axis)}
/>
```

## Drag controls

Use `createDragControls` to start a drag from an external element, like a drag handle:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { motion, createDragControls } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    const controls = createDragControls()
</script>

<button onpointerdown={(e) => controls.start(e)}>Drag handle</button>

<motion.div drag dragControls={controls} dragListener={false} />
```

Setting `dragListener={false}` prevents the element itself from initiating the drag — only the external handle will work.

The `start` method accepts an optional options object:

```ts
controls.start(event, { snapToCursor: true })
```

## While dragging

Use `whileDrag` to animate the element while a drag gesture is active:

```svelte
<motion.div
    drag
    whileDrag={{ scale: 1.1, boxShadow: '0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,0.2)' }}
/>
```

## Propagation

By default, drag events propagate to parent drag components. Set `dragPropagation={false}` to prevent this:

```svelte
<motion.div drag>
    <motion.div drag dragPropagation={false} />
</motion.div>
```

## Callbacks

Drag provides several lifecycle callbacks. Each receives the native `PointerEvent` and a `DragInfo` object:

```svelte
<motion.div
    drag
    onDragStart={(event, info) => console.log('Start', info.point)}
    onDrag={(event, info) => console.log('Move', info.delta)}
    onDragEnd={(event, info) => console.log('End', info.velocity)}
    onDragTransitionEnd={() => console.log('Momentum settled')}
/>
```

### DragInfo

The `info` object passed to drag callbacks contains:

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `point` | `{ x, y }` | Current pointer position |
| `delta` | `{ x, y }` | Change since the last drag event (per-frame delta) |
| `offset` | `{ x, y }` | Cumulative transform offset since drag start |
| `velocity` | `{ x, y }` | Current velocity in px/s |

## Behavior

`drag` runs on Motion's own drag controller, so these details match Motion:

- **Default styles.** A draggable element gets `user-select: none`, `draggable="false"` and a `touch-action` for its axis: `none` for `drag`, `pan-y` for `drag="x"` and `pan-x` for `drag="y"`. They're set inline, so they win over the same properties in your CSS classes or `style`. Set `dragListener={false}` to opt out.
- **Pointer cancel.** A `pointercancel` (for example, the browser taking over a touch for scrolling) ends the drag like a `pointerup`. `onDragEnd` fires with the release velocity and momentum still runs.
- **Re-grabbing outside the constraints.** If you catch an element while it's outside its constraints, it moves back toward the edge as you drag, then follows the pointer at the `dragElastic` rate.
- **Element constraints.** Resizing the window, the element or the constraint element moves the element to the same relative position within the new constraints. Only the first constraint element is watched for resizes. If you swap `dragConstraints` to a different element, the new bounds apply, but resizing only the new element won't reposition anything.
- **Drag controls.** Every element that receives the same `dragControls` is driven by `start`, `cancel` and `stop`. `stop()` ends a live drag with momentum and `cancel()` ends it without. Neither does anything once the drag is over, so neither stops an animation that's already running after release.

## Vanilla drag()

`drag()` makes plain elements draggable without a motion component (Motion 14.4). It takes an element, a list of elements or a CSS selector, and returns a function that removes the gesture:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { drag } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    let track = $state<HTMLElement | null>(null)
    let knob = $state<HTMLElement | null>(null)

    $effect(() => {
        if (!knob || !track) return
        return drag(knob, { axis: 'x', constraints: track })
    })
</script>

<div bind:this={track}>
    <div bind:this={knob}></div>
</div>
```

While attached, `drag()` sets `user-select: none`, `touch-action` (`none`, or `pan-y`/`pan-x` with an `axis`) and `draggable="false"` on the element. The cleanup function restores them.

### Options

| Option | Type | Default | Description |
|--------|------|---------|-------------|
| `axis` | `'x' \| 'y'` | both axes | Restrict dragging to one axis |
| `constraints` | `{ top?, left?, right?, bottom? } \| Element` | — | Pixel bounds relative to the origin, or an element to stay within |
| `elastic` | `number \| { top?, left?, right?, bottom? }` | `0.35` | How far, from `0` to `1`, the element can move outside its constraints |
| `momentum` | `boolean` | `true` | Carry the release velocity into an inertia animation |
| `transition` | `InertiaOptions` | — | Options for the inertia animation on release |
| `snapToOrigin` | `boolean \| 'x' \| 'y'` | `false` | Animate back to the origin on release |
| `directionLock` | `boolean` | `false` | Lock to the first axis the pointer moves along |
| `propagation` | `boolean` | `false` | Let drags start on draggable parents too |
| `onStart` / `onMove` / `onEnd` | `(event, info) => void` | — | Drag lifecycle callbacks, with the same `info` as [DragInfo](#draginfo) |
| `onDirectionLock` | `(axis: 'x' \| 'y') => void` | — | Fires when `directionLock` picks an axis |
| `onTransitionEnd` | `() => void` | — | Fires when the release animation settles |
| `onMeasureConstraints` | `(constraints) => constraints \| void` | — | Called when element constraints are measured; return a box to override them |

### With animate() and animateLayout()

The element's `x` and `y` are motion values shared with [`animate()`](/docs/use-animate) and [`animateLayout()`](/docs/animate-layout). An element moved by `animate()` keeps its offset when a drag starts, and you can animate it again after a drag:

```ts
await animate(knob, { x: 120 })
const stop = drag(knob) // the drag continues from x: 120

// later, spring it home
animate(knob, { x: 0 }, { type: 'spring' })
```

### In a scope

Called inside a [`useScopeMotion`](/docs/scope-motion) or `scopeMotion` setup, a selector resolves within the scope's root, callbacks run in the scope, and the gesture is removed when the scope stops:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { drag, useScopeMotion } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    const scope = useScopeMotion(() => {
        drag('.handle', { axis: 'y' })
    })
</script>

<ul {@attach scope}>
    <li class="handle">Only handles inside this list are draggable</li>
</ul>
```

### motion components

A motion component that already has a `drag` prop doesn't need `drag()`; use its drag props instead. As in Motion, `drag()` doesn't check for this. If you pass such an element anyway, `drag()` attaches a second drag gesture, and the element still moves only once: Motion's drag lock lets one gesture at a time take the pointer, and both gestures share the element's `x` and `y`.

## Props

### Drag

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `drag` | `boolean \| 'x' \| 'y'` | `false` | Enable dragging. `true` for both axes, or restrict to one axis |
| `dragConstraints` | `{ top?, left?, right?, bottom? } \| HTMLElement` | — | Pixel bounds or element reference to constrain drag |
| `dragElastic` | `number` | `0.35` | Elasticity when overdragging. `0` = hard stop, `1` = full stretch |
| `dragMomentum` | `boolean` | `true` | Apply momentum after release |
| `dragTransition` | `DragTransition` | — | Physics configuration for momentum and boundaries |
| `dragSnapToOrigin` | `boolean` | `false` | Spring back to starting position on release |
| `dragDirectionLock` | `boolean` | `false` | Lock to first detected axis of movement |
| `dragPropagation` | `boolean` | `true` | Allow drag events to bubble to parent drag elements |
| `dragListener` | `boolean` | `true` | Enable the built-in drag listener on the element |
| `dragControls` | `DragControls` | — | External controls for programmatic drag initiation |
| `whileDrag` | `MotionWhileDrag` | — | Animation target while drag is active |

### Callbacks

| Prop | Type | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| `onDragStart` | `(event, info) => void` | Fires when drag begins |
| `onDrag` | `(event, info) => void` | Fires on every drag movement |
| `onDragEnd` | `(event, info) => void` | Fires when drag ends |
| `onDirectionLock` | `(axis: 'x' \| 'y') => void` | Fires when direction is locked |
| `onDragTransitionEnd` | `() => void` | Fires when post-drag momentum settles |

### DragTransition

| Property | Type | Default | Description |
|----------|------|---------|-------------|
| `bounceStiffness` | `number` | `200` with elasticity, `1000000` without | Spring stiffness at constraint boundaries |
| `bounceDamping` | `number` | `40` with elasticity, `10000000` without | Damping at constraint boundaries |
| `power` | `number` | `0.8` | Momentum power factor |
| `timeConstant` | `number` | `750` | Exponential decay time constant (ms) |
| `restDelta` | `number` | `1` | Distance threshold for "at rest" |
| `restSpeed` | `number` | `10` | Velocity threshold for boundary spring rest |
| `modifyTarget` | `(target: number) => number` | — | Adjust the calculated inertia target |
| `min` | `number` | — | Minimum value for axis |
| `max` | `number` | — | Maximum value for axis |

